Subject: fix(sandbox): do not report success for a profile import that replaced nothing

A provider profile can silently stop tracking its source file, permanently,
and the only symptom is an agent denied access the profile grants.

ImportProfile deletes the existing profile and reimports it. The delete result
is discarded, and an import refused with "already exists" is treated as a
parallel-import race: success, and the new file's hash is written to the local
cache. That is right only when the delete succeeded.

A gateway refuses to delete a profile a live sandbox still references. There
the delete fails, the import is refused because the old profile is still
there, and the gateway keeps serving it — while ImportProfile reports success
and caches the *new* file's hash. Every later run then takes the fast path,
skips the import entirely, and reports success against a profile that was
never replaced. Nothing mentions the profile again.

The consequence is not cosmetic: a profile is a sandbox's network and binary
policy. Observed on a long-lived shared gateway where an unrelated sandbox
from weeks earlier held a reference. The installed profile still allowed
binaries under /usr/local/lib/node_modules while the agent runs from
/usr/lib/node_modules, so every model call was denied, and the source file on
disk said otherwise.

Keep the delete result and use it to tell the two cases apart. A refused
import after a successful delete is still a race and still succeeds. A refused
import after a failed delete returns an error naming the delete failure, and
leaves the cache alone so the next run retries rather than skipping. The error
names `openshell provider profile update`, which replaces a profile in place
and is the way out when delete is refused.

One existing test's fake openshell failed *every* invocation with "already
exists", including the delete, which conflated the two cases this change
separates. Its fake now fails only the import, which is the race it describes.

Breadboard note: patches Go source, so it belongs to the build patch list in
deploy/scripts/05i-build-fullsend.sh rather than the mirror list. Written to
be sent upstream as-is. Add a DCO sign-off before submitting it as a
human-authored change.

